{:6300} ' &,62: set time
' &,62,hour,minute: hour as in d1$: 12, 1-11 = AM; 92, 81-91 = PM
' The CIA hours register (BCD) should show the same two digits as the hour,
' and d1$ (rebuilt by the ML on the next IRQ) should too.
' 0 and 80 aren't valid CIA hours: see what happens.
'
' This changes the real BBS clock: d1$ is saved, and the time is restored
' afterwards (seconds since the test started are lost).
' Minute 30 is used so that 12 AM doesn't trigger the midnight date
' rollover in irq2 (hours=$12 and minutes=0).
' Each &,62 may take 1 minute off the user's time remaining.
'
' CIA #1 time of day: 56331 ($dc0b) hours, 56330 ($dc0a) minutes,
' 56328 ($dc08) tenths. Reading hours freezes these registers until
' tenths is read, so tenths is read last.
	&"{f6}&{comma}62 Set Time{f6:2}"
	lp=1:&"This changes the BBS clock (it is restored afterwards). Continue{question} ":gosub 96:if a=. then return
	s$=d1$:hx$="0123456789abcdef":h$="1201119281910080"
	&"{f6}Before{colon} d1${equal}{quotation}{pound}$s{quotation}{f6:2}"
	for i=0 to 7:h=val(mid$(h$,i*2+1,2)):e$=mid$(h$,i*2+1,2):if i>5 then e$="??"
	c=30:gosub {:sub.test_settim}:next
	h=val(mid$(s$,8,2)):c=val(mid$(s$,10,2)):&,62,h,c
	&"{f6}Restored{colon} d1${equal}{quotation}"+d1$+"{quotation}{f6}":return
{:sub.test_settim}
' set the time, wait for the next IRQ, then read the CIA clock and d1$
	&,62,h,c:for w=1 to 100:next
	hv=peek(56331):mv=peek(56330):tv=peek(56328)
	r$=mid$(hx$,int(hv/16)+1,1)+mid$(hx$,(hv and 15)+1,1)
	&"&{comma}62{comma}{pound}!h{comma}{pound}!c{colon} CIA hours ${pound}$r (expect {pound}$e){comma} d1$ hour "+mid$(d1$,8,2)+"{f6}"
	return

